FasTest::Go is a test suite implementor that speeds up test development by providing xUnit-like features for RegTest scripts.

In short, FasTest finds and calls your test functions for you.

Using FasTest
Call FasTest::Go at the end of the test script. FasTest::Go replaces RegTest::Test Id.

FasTest calls each Private::Test... function.
Setup/Teardown functions are supported.
If a test function ('test case') throws an exception, it is caught and remaining test functions are still called.
By default, this is suppressed in Secview so that you can debug, but enabled in RAMS.
Enable by passing Protect:=true.
Pass Assert Is Guard:=True to make assertion failure exit the test case.
Test script with FasTest example
A typical test script with FasTest looks like this:


 Link( "_LIB FasTest" );
 Private::Test My Fn = Func()
 {
     // NB. No 'print' statement required, FasTest identifies each test function
     ...
 }
 Private::Test My Other Fn = Func()
 ...
 
 @FasTest::Go( "my regtg", "kerb", "kerb" ); // NB. no 'main' function
See also: Example: FasTest.

Setup/TearDown
If defined in your script, FasTest::Go will also call the following functions:

Private::Setup suite
once at the start
Private::Setup
before every test case
Private::Teardown
after every test case (as long as Setup did not throw)
Private::Teardown suite
once at the end (as long as Setup suite did not throw)
Specific test types
FasTest can implement blessed model tests. Set the first argument to test type, eg "Price", and pass Model := your model name, or array of model names.

Test case parameterization
Split tests into independent cases using the function comment FasTest-params on your test function. Each case is executed independently, including setup/teardown and exception handling (if using Protect := True ). NOTE: If your test case params depend on private variables that you have defined in your setup suite, the test will not work. Define your private variables right on top in your script(not in setup suite) and then your test will be able to find the pvt vars.

Parameterized test example
The following will check four test cases for Size:


Link( "_LIB RegTest Fns" );
Link( "_LIB FasTest" );
/****************************************************************
**  Routine: Private::Test Size
**
**  FasTest-params: Private::Size Cases
****************************************************************/
Private::Test Size = Func(
    Any( Input ),
    Double( Expected Size ),
)
Returns()
{
    Assert( Sprintf( "%s has correct size", DataTypeOf( Input ) ), Size( Input ) == Expected Size );
};
// Parameter list, as an array.  Could be a table, TDS, etc. 
// Note everything here must be defined ahead of calling @FasTest::Go
Private::Size Cases = [
    [ "",           0 ],
    [ "foo",        3 ],
    [ {||},         0 ],
    [ {| a := 1 |}, 1 ],
];
Running the script with Private::Test Size above produces the output

[ 1/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ 0
1 ASSERTION PASSED: String has correct size
[ 2/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ 1
2 ASSERTION PASSED: String has correct size
[ 3/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ 2
3 ASSERTION PASSED: Structure has correct size
[ 4/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ 3
4 ASSERTION PASSED: Structure has correct size
Labeling cases
The parameter value list's indices or keys are used to name the cases: eg

 Private::Size Cases = {|
    "Size of empty string"       := [ "",           0 ],
    "Size of non-empty string"   := [ "foo",        3 ],
    "Size of empty structure"    := [ {||},         0 ],
    "Size of non-empty structure":= [ {| a := 1 |}, 1 ],
 |};
would result in

[ 1/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ Size of empty string
1 ASSERTION PASSED: String has correct size
[ 2/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ Size of empty structure
2 ASSERTION PASSED: Structure has correct size
[ 3/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ Size of non-empty string
3 ASSERTION PASSED: String has correct size
[ 4/ 4] ------------ Test Size $ Size of non-empty structure
4 ASSERTION PASSED: Structure has correct size
Defining parameter value lists
Parameter list identifiers must be one of:

a private variable or function, defined at the script top level
a publicly scoped variable or function
If the parameter list is a function it must take no arguments and return a suitable value. It will be called once for each test function it is applied to.

Suitable values include most subscriptable data types: eg

Array( Array() )
Array( Structure() )
TDS
NOTE: Parameterization happens before calling any test cases or setup functions (e.g. Setup Suite).

In particular, the following will redbox:


Private::Setup Suite = Func(
)
Returns()
{
    Private::Params = [ [ "foo" ] ]; // error: Access of an uninitialized variable 'Params'
};
//...
/****************************************************************
**  Routine: Private::Test That Will Not Parameterize
**
**  FasTest-params: Private::Params
****************************************************************/
Private::Test That Will Not Parameterize = Func( //...
Test case generation
There is support for procedurally generating test case Lambdas. This can also be applied after an initial parameterization.

See: Example: FasTest Parameterize, Test: FasTest Parameterize

SecView gravy
Alt-X, FasTest Run
Select specific test cases to run, for the currently open script (rather than running all cases)
Alt-X, FasTest Rerun Failed
Rerun last test. Only failed, or new cases will be run.
Generate a test script for your library: see boilerplate FAQ. Select the test type '"Slang with FasTest"'.

Example SVE Defaults setup

 Link( "_LIB FasTest" );
 _ = Sve::FasTest Run;
 SveKeyExpression( WK_CTRLSHFF,  "@Sve::FasTest Rerun Failed()" );
 SveKeyExpression( WK_CTRLSHFT,  "@Sve::FasTest Run()" );
(see also SVE Defaults FAQ)

From the commandline
Example of running only the named test cases from secexpr:


secexpr --safe "my test script" "Test Case1" "Test Case2" -s "_UT FasTest"
Questions and answers
I don't need Setup/Teardown, can I remove them?
Yes, they will only be called if present. (Lint checks that their names are correct.)

What order are test functions (cases) called in?
Test cases are called in alphanumeric order.

What if I have other functions in my script not called 'Test...'?
FasTest ignores these. It will also ignore Test functions that take non-optional positional arguments. Lint will inform you of this.

What is the object database used for a test case?
RegProd is the default object database.

How do I change the object database?
Pass the object database to FasTest::Go:

@FasTest::Go(
 ...  
 Database := Database( ... )  );
What about diddles?
Each test case is wrapped in an Eval block, as is the whole test suite. This block also encompasses Private::Setup, if defined. So you can set diddles in Private::Setup or Private::Setup Suite.

Can I skip a test function?
To always skip a test function, add the function comment FasTest: skip to the function header.

To always skip all test functions except for a particular subset, add the function comment FasTest: focus to the function header. This is particularly useful for long test scripts, or test scripts that require entitlements you dont have.

Lint will detect and warn about the unused function (unless you use SharePrivateScope in your test script).

Alternatively, use Alt-X, FasTest Run in SecView.

How do I provide a standardised wrapper fn in the cases of when scoped wrapping matters?
Use the FasTest-wrap-with: _fnname_ feature.

Example:

/****************************************************************
**  Routine: Private::Test Fn that needs to be wrapped
**
**  FasTest-wrap-with: Private::Wrapper Fn 
****************************************************************/
Private::Test Fn that needs to be wrapped....

/****************************************************************
**  Routine: Private::Wrapper Fn
**
**  Standard scoped mocks and other stuff for tests in this script
****************************************************************/
Private::Wrapper Fn = Func(
    Slang( Test Fn ) // the wrapper receives the function that executes the test case as a slang arg
                     // this already includes all the encapsulation of args etc required
                     // with parameterized test cases
)
Returns( Slang() )  
{
    Return( \ -> RegTestStubFunction( your standard mocks ){ @Test Fn() } );
};
Wrapper functions can be from the private or from a library scope, which is useful for standard wrappers

Note: If using FasTest-wrap-with in conjunction with FasTest-params, any parameters injected by your wrap function must come after any bound by your params, e.g.


/****************************************************************
**  Routine: Private::Test X
**
**  FasTest-params: Private::X Params
**  FasTest-wrap-with: Private::Wrapper Fn
****************************************************************/
Private::X Params = TableInit( [
    [ "A", "B" ],
    [   1,   2 ],
    [   3,   4 ],
] );
Private::Test X = Func(
    Double( A ),
    Double( B ),
    String( Str ),  // must come after A and B
)
{ ... }

/****************************************************************
**  Routine: Private::Wrapper Fn
**
**  Standard scoped mocks and other stuff for tests in this script
****************************************************************/
Private::Wrapper Fn = Func(
    Slang( Test Fn )
)
Returns( Slang() )  
{
    Return( \ -> RegTestStubFunction( your standard mocks ){ @Test Fn( "some str" ) } );
};
